  "raw": "{\n  \"audits\": [\n    {\n      \"questionId\": \"q001\",\n      \"decision\": \"accept\",\n      \"assessedIds\": [\"cnm_h105\"],\n      \"supportIds\": [],\n      \"routes\": [],\n      \"derivedAnswer\": \"B — Parallel to each other and congruent\",\n      \"mathematicalReason\": \"A prism has two congruent polygonal bases lying in parallel planes. This defining relationship is within cnm_h105's scope of understanding prism structure and its constituent faces.\",\n      \"candidateFindings\": [],\n      \"needsNewNodes\": [],\n      \"limitations\": [],\n      \"figureEvidence\": [\n        {\n          \"imageSlot\": 1,\n          \"fact\": \"The drawing represents a pentagonal prism, with two corresponding pentagonal end faces joined by lateral edges. The general relationship between the bases follows from the prism definition, not from measuring the drawing.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"questionId\": \"q002\",\n      \"decision\": \"accept\",\n      \"assessedIds\": [\"cnm_258\"],\n      \"supportIds\": [\"cnm_174\"],\n      \"routes\": [],\n      \"derivedAnswer\": \"65\",\n      \"mathematicalReason\": \"For the depicted right circular cone, the positive slant height is l = sqrt(12^2 + 5^2) = 13. Its lateral area is πrl = π·5·13 = 65π, so k = 65. Cone lateral area is the assessed procedure in cnm_258; the Pythagorean theorem appropriately supports finding the slant height.\",\n      \"candidateFindings\": [],\n      \"needsNewNodes\": [],\n      \"limitations\": [],\n      \"figureEvidence\": [\n        {\n          \"imageSlot\": 2,\n          \"fact\": \"The cone is depicted with its apex above the base center, a central height labeled h = 12, and a segment from the center to the rim labeled r = 5.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"questionId\": \"q003\",\n      \"decision\": \"accept\",\n      \"assessedIds\": [\"cnm_p160\"],\n      \"supportIds\": [],\n      \"routes\": [],\n      \"derivedAnswer\": \"8, 12, 6\",\n      \"mathematicalReason\": \"Two opposite rectangular faces contribute 4 + 4 = 8 vertices and 4 + 4 edges; four connecting edges give 12 edges altogether. The solid has two opposite end faces and four other faces, totaling 6. These are precisely the rectangular-prism elements covered by cnm_p160.\",\n      \"candidateFindings\": [],\n      \"needsNewNodes\": [],\n      \"limitations\": [],\n      \"figureEvidence\": [\n        {\n          \"imageSlot\": 3,\n          \"fact\": \"The rectangular-prism drawing has two offset rectangular outlines joined at corresponding corners. Crossings of projected edges inside the drawing do not represent additional solid vertices.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"questionId\": \"q004\",\n      \"decision\": \"accept\",\n      \"assessedIds\": [\"cnm_251\"],\n      \"supportIds\": [\"cnm_174\"],\n      \"routes\": [],\n      \"derivedAnswer\": \"3\",\n      \"mathematicalReason\": \"Let M be the perpendicular foot from O to AB. The perpendicular from the center bisects the chord, so AM = 8/2 = 4. Since OA = 5, the right triangle OMA gives OM = sqrt(5^2 - 4^2) = 3, taking the nonnegative value because OM is a distance. This directly applies cnm_251, with cnm_174 supporting the calculation.\",\n      \"candidateFindings\": [],\n      \"needsNewNodes\": [],\n      \"limitations\": [],\n      \"figureEvidence\": [\n        {\n          \"imageSlot\": 4,\n          \"fact\": \"The circle has center O, a radius labeled 5, and chord AB labeled 8. A right-angle marker shows that the line through O meets AB perpendicularly.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"questionId\": \"q005\",\n      \"decision\": \"accept\",\n      \"assessedIds\": [\"cnm_h105\"],\n      \"supportIds\": [],\n      \"routes\": [],\n      \"derivedAnswer\": \"5\",\n      \"mathematicalReason\": \"A triangular prism has two triangular bases and one lateral face for each of the triangle's three edges. Thus it has 2 + 3 = 5 faces. Counting these prism components falls directly within cnm_h105.\",\n      \"candidateFindings\": [],\n      \"needsNewNodes\": [],\n      \"limitations\": [],\n      \"figureEvidence\": [\n        {\n          \"imageSlot\": 5,\n          \"fact\": \"The drawing shows two corresponding triangular end faces joined by three lateral edges, forming the three quadrilateral lateral faces.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"questionId\": \"q006\",\n      \"decision\": \"accept\",\n      \"assessedIds\": [\"cnm_056\"],\n      \"supportIds\": [],\n      \"routes\": [],\n      \"derivedAnswer\": \"6\",\n      \"mathematicalReason\": \"The qualifying angles are AOB, AOC, AOD, BOC, BOD, and COD. Each distinct pair of rays forms one angle within the stated sector of less than 180°, giving 6 angles. Direct enumeration uses the angle concept and notation in cnm_056; a combinations formula is not required.\",\n      \"candidateFindings\": [],\n      \"needsNewNodes\": [],\n      \"limitations\": [],\n      \"figureEvidence\": [\n        {\n          \"imageSlot\": 6,\n          \"fact\": \"Four distinct rays share endpoint O and appear in the order OA, OB, OC, OD, with OB and OC inside the sector bounded by OA and OD.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"questionId\": \"q007\",\n      \"decision\": \"accept\",\n      \"assessedIds\": [\"cnm_h110\"],\n      \"supportIds\": [\"cnm_p089\"],\n      \"routes\": [],\n      \"derivedAnswer\": \"40\",\n      \"mathematicalReason\": \"The lateral surface unfolds into a rectangle with side lengths equal to the base circumference, 10, and the height, 4. Therefore its area is 10·4 = 40 square units, without adding either base. This matches cnm_h110's explicit coverage of using unfolded-surface data, with rectangle area from cnm_p089 as support.\",\n      \"candidateFindings\": [],\n      \"needsNewNodes\": [],\n      \"limitations\": [],\n      \"figureEvidence\": [\n        {\n          \"imageSlot\": 7,\n          \"fact\": \"The cylinder is labeled 'Base perimeter 10' and 'Height 4'. The rectangular unfolding is stated in the question rather than shown in the image.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"questionId\": \"q008\",\n      \"decision\": \"accept\",\n      \"assessedIds\": [\"cnm_084\"],\n      \"supportIds\": [\"cnm_p077\", \"cnm_p089\"],\n      \"routes\": [],\n      \"derivedAnswer\": \"-1, 2, 12\",\n      \"mathematicalReason\": \"AB is horizontal and BC is vertical. The fourth vertex therefore shares A's x-coordinate and C's y-coordinate, giving D = (-1, 2). The side lengths are 3 - (-1) = 4 and 2 - (-1) = 3, so S = 4·3 = 12 square units. Coordinate-based vertex, length, and area determination fits cnm_084; rectangle properties and the area formula appropriately support it.\",\n      \"candidateFindings\": [],\n      \"needsNewNodes\": [],\n      \"limitations\": [],\n      \"figureEvidence\": [\n        {\n          \"imageSlot\": 8,\n          \"fact\": \"The grid depicts an axis-parallel rectangle with A(-1, -1) at the lower left, B(3, -1) at the lower right, C(3, 2) at the upper right, and D at the upper left.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}",
